persona #self.first# dries apples and seals crocks in a loft under the slates: talkative, curious and dry as a good ring of apple, tidy to a fault, and gives the coming weather by the catch in that chest, which is never, ever a cough.

words
oath! = Sulphur and string | Cores and cobwebs | By the drying racks
saying! = Sugar forgives, damp never does | Nothing keeps that wasn't picked sound | Seal it hot or eat it now
loft = the loft | the apple loft under the slates
strings = the apple strings | rings of apple threaded on twine
racks = the drying racks | the slatted racks
crocks = the crocks | the stone crocks on the bottom shelf
wax = a lid of wax | the sealing wax
sugar = sugar | good white sugar
sulphur = the sulphur pot | a pinch of sulphur on the coals
chest = this chest of mine | my weather glass of a chest
catch = a catch | a little rattle
stew = mutton stew | mutton and pearl barley, slow-done
board = the draughts board | the old board with the chipped black king
flies = the flies | the summer flies
rats = the rats | rats in the eaves
jam = jam | a pot of plum jam
windfalls = windfalls | bruised windfalls at a penny the basket
apples = apples | good keeping apples
vinegar = vinegar | sharp vinegar
broom = the broom | my good broom

any
opinion @1 church mixed 0 : Chapel sees me some Restdays, not all. If the Bright One needs a word, my loft's easy found. | A good sermon's like a good jam: short, sweet, and nobody talks through it.
opinion @1 city for 1 : Smoke, rain and four hundred steps. Offer me an orchard in the sun and I'll still say no. | Born under these slates. I'll go under them, and probably still complaining about the damp.
opinion @1 festivals for 1 : Every door in the Commons open on one afternoon, and nobody rushing. That suits me. | A festival's good for jam. Everybody wants something sweet when there's music.
opinion @1 houses against 2 [self.age>=40] : Grayrock runs the wharf the way a cat runs a henhouse. I've lost too much down there to say otherwise. | Don't ask me to drink to House Grayrock. I'll drink, but not to that.
opinion @2 houses mixed 0 : The Houses feed Ridgemere and pinch it, and sometimes in one week. | Fell sends the best apples in the realm down that road. I'll say that much for one House.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 [self.role=merchant] : Anyone who's kept a counter through a wet winter knows the price of a dry shelf. | Keep a shop and you learn every cost there is, and a few more nobody warned you of.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : New arrivals have stomachs, same as the born, and I'll sell anybody a jar. | Newcomers ask questions. I like a wolf who asks questions. I usually have answers.
opinion @1 road against 1 : The orchards send their apples by cart, and every week the cart's later. Fruit doesn't wait. | Each day a cart dawdles is a bruise on every apple in it.
opinion @2 road against 2 [world.wagons=raided] : Thieves had a wagon, and some highwayman's eating my apples raw. They're cookers. Serves him right.
opinion @1 strangers for 1 : Passing folk want food that lasts the journey, and they pay in coin and news both. | Every stranger who climbs my stairs carries a story I haven't heard yet.
opinion @1 watch for 1 : Not one string's gone missing since the night patrols began. Good enough for me. | I give the night watch a dried apple ring each as they pass. It's the cheapest lock I own.
opinion @1 drink mixed 0 : A cider's a fine thing, from my own apples or anyone's. Three's a mess on the stairs. | I'll take a cup. I'll not take the headache, so one's my rule.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. Mind the strings over the door, you'll wear one. | #greet#. In from the wet. Smells of apples in here; it always will.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. A new face on my stairs. Passing through, or staying to suffer the rain, I can't tell. | #greet#. A stranger. Good. Strangers ask the interesting questions.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.job=merchant] : #greet#. Talk while I tie, if you can stand the sulphur. | #greet#. I've a crock half sealed, so you'll get half my attention.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. I've been up checking the racks since the bells. | Morning. Dry air for once, so I'm cheerful. It won't last.
line @5 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. I was counting crocks. I lose count every night at about forty. | Evening. Listen to the loft creak; it's settling down to sleep.
line @6 greet [pair.band=friends|family] : Good timing. The board's set and nobody's there to lose to me. | Good, company. The apples have heard all my news twice and never answer back.
line @7 greet [here.weather=rain|fog] : #greet#. My chest told me you'd come in wet. It's never wrong. | #greet#. Drip on the mat, please, not on the racks.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Sit. There's stew, and there's a chair that isn't covered in strings. | You're back. Shoes off, please, I've just done the floor.
line @1 again.open : Back so quick means news or a forgotten glove. | You again, before I've even swept up after the last time.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Here, a minute. I want your opinion and then I'll ignore it. | Hold on there. I'm headed your way, more or less. Keep me company.
line @1 greet.return : #greet# yourself. Nice to be noticed. | And to you. Duck the strings.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Took your time. Sit. | Come here, sit. You look like you've news.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Talk while I tie; these paws work without me. | Hello. Excuse the sulphur, it's a working day.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening to you too. Late, isn't it. | Evening. Softly; the loft's asleep.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Close the door on the way out, the damp follows folk in. | #farewell#. Next sweet tooth you get, you know my stairs.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. The corner gutter runs deep after dark; step wide. | Good night. Crocks to count before I sleep.
line @1 farewell.return : And to you. Don't let the rain in your collar. | #farewell#. My door's here when you want it.
line @2 farewell.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Go on, then. Same time tomorrow, and bring better luck at draughts. | Night. Take a ring of apple for the walk.
line @1 react.news : Is that so? Now that I didn't expect. Go on. | Start at the start, and slowly. I'm all ears.
line @1 react.news.new : That's new. Whose tongue did it come off? | That never climbed as high as the loft. Who's saying it?
line @1 react.news.known : A customer had a go at it this morning, but told it worse. | I'd heard. Your telling's better, mind.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Hm. One telling doesn't sell me. Bring me two. | That one's put on weight climbing the stairs, I'd say.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : That's a poor thing to hear. Somebody's carrying that tonight, and I'm sorry. | Ah. That's bad. That's the kind of news that sits in the chest.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Good. I'll send a pot of jam round. That's what jam's for. | That's cheered me up, and I wasn't expecting to be cheered today.
line @1 answer.how : Fine, weather aside. I always set the weather aside. | Busy. Tidy. Mostly dry. Can't ask more.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Strings to thread and crocks to seal and only two paws to do it with. | Busier than a fly on a jam lid, and about as welcome.
line @1 answer.how.poorly [here.weather=rain|fog] : Well enough. Ignore the rattle. That's the forecast, not a cold. | Damp's in, and I can feel every drop of it. I'm fine.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Wet to the ears, I see. | How's your week been? Honestly, not the shop answer.
line @1 sympathise : That's hard, and I won't pretend otherwise. Sit, have a cup. | I'm sorry for it. Words won't fix it, but I can listen all afternoon.
line @1 reassure : A week and one dry day, and most things look smaller. | You'll come through. You're the sort who comes out the far end.
line @1 decline : Kind of you, but a batch on the coals won't mind itself. | Not this time. The loft needs me, and the loft doesn't take excuses.
line @1 accept_decline : That's fair. Another time, then. | No harm done. I'll ask again when you've less on.
line @1 thank : That's kind. Kindness goes in my book, and you're in it now. | #thanks#. You've saved me a climb up the stairs.
line @1 deflect_thanks : Pfft. Worth less than a pot of jam. | Don't thank me, it was going spare anyway.
line @1 preclose : Racks need me before the damp gets them first. | Hark at me, rattling on. Go, go.
line @2 preclose [self.working=yes] : A crock's cooling and wants its wax, story or no story. | The sulphur needs a stir. We'll stop there.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on. We'll finish it another day. | Right you are. Duck on your way out.
line @1 tease [pair.band=friends] : You played that draught like you were paying for the board by the move. | That's a brave hat for a wet day.
line @1 tease.return [pair.band=friends] : Says the wolf who lost three games running. | You can laugh. My chest says rain by noon, and you've no hood.
line @1 ack : #bc_right# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_it_does#
line @1 give.advice : Pick your apples dry and your friends sober. | Never put a lid on anything still warm. Food or temper.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Stay inside the gate a while. Sensible wolves are keeping off the road. | Not past the gate now. Not for a whole cart of apples.
line @1 congratulate : Good. Well done. I'll put a pot of something aside for you. | That's earned. I'd shake your paw if mine weren't sticky.
scene not_a_cough
when topic=weather|health here.weather=rain|fog
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Hear that? Not a cough. My chest telling me it'll rain till Restday. | There, that little rattle. Rain by evening, and heavier than this. | My chest's been going since dawn. That means the damp's in for a week.
b : Sounded rather like coughing. | How can you tell? | Have a healer listen to it.
a : Everybody says that. It's a weather glass. It just happens to live in me. | It goes thick before the rain comes, and dry when it clears. Better than any sailor's nose. | I see somebody. They say rest and honey. I say I'm busy and the honey's for jam.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_clever# | #bc_ha#

scene the_cough_denied
when topic=health
does ask.how / answer.how / insist / ack
a : Don't start. I know what you're going to ask about my chest. | Before you say anything, I'm not ill.
b : My lips were shut, honestly. | You don't sound well, though.
a : About to open, they were. Everyone's do. It's damp, not disease. I'm right as rain, and I know rain. | I sound like weather. Weather's not illness. Ask the sky if it's poorly.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene threading_the_rings
when topic=work self.job=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Forty strings this morning, every ring a finger thick. Any thinner and they go to leather. | I thread #strings# with a bone needle older than the loft. Same needle, same knot. | The trick with apple rings is the cut. Thick as a finger, cored clean, and in the lime water quick.
b : Why a finger thick? | How old is that needle? | Lime water? What for?
a : Thin dries to leather, thick goes mouldy in the middle. A finger's the honest width. | Older than me. It came with the first crock I ever bought, and it's outlived the crock. | Stops them going brown. Folk won't buy a brown ring even if it tastes the same.
b : #bc_clever# | #bc_good# | #bc_clever#

scene sulphur_smoke
when topic=work self.job=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : I fume the apples with #sulphur# before they go up. Keeps the colour and keeps the flies off. | Everyone on the street knows when I'm fuming. They can smell it three doors down.
b : That sounds like it'd catch your chest. | Do they complain?
a : It does, and I pretend it doesn't, and we get along. | They complain in autumn and buy in spring. Fair's fair.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene wax_on_the_crocks
when topic=work self.job=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : #wax.capitalize# on every crock, poured hot, then a bladder skin tied over that. Nothing gets in. | I seal the jam while it's too hot to touch. It hurts. It's supposed to.
b : Why both, wax and skin? | Why so hot?
a : Wax keeps the air out, skin keeps the mice off the wax. Belt and braces. | Hot jam seals itself. Lukewarm jam grows a beard by spring.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_ha#

scene sugar_price_sermon
when topic=prices self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint.agree / tell.self / ack
a : Sugar's gone up again. I could preserve the city with what that costs. | You know what the dearest thing in my loft is? Not the apples. The sugar.
b : Everything's going up. | Can't you use honey?
a : It is. But sugar's the one I can't do without. Jam without sugar is just a sad stew. | Honey, for some. It changes the taste and the keeping both. Customers notice, and they say so.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene windfall_morning
when topic=harvest|season here.season=autumn self.job=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Bought six baskets of #windfalls# this morning. Bruised, but bruised is fine for jam. | Autumn. Heavy carts, sore arms, and a purse with a smile on it. | Windfall season. Every orchard in the realm wants rid of its bruised ones, and I want them.
b : Bruised ones make good jam, then? | Happy purse? | Why the bruised ones?
a : Better than good. Cut the bruise out and they've more taste than the pretty ones. | Windfalls are cheap and I'm patient. Put those two together and the purse smiles. | Cheap, and the flavour's all there under the bruise. Customers shop with their eyes. Jam doesn't need eyes.
b : #bc_clever# | #bc_ha# | #bc_true_enough#

scene wet_autumn_racks
when topic=weather|work here.season=autumn here.weather=rain|fog self.job=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / insist / ack
a : Three days of fog in drying season. The rings on the top rack have gone soft as wet bread. | A wet autumn's a preserver's nightmare. I've had the brazier going in the loft all night.
b : You'll save most of them, surely. | Won't the brazier dry them?
a : Most. The rest go to jam, and I'll say the jam was always the plan. | It dries them, and smokes them, and coughs me. I'll take two out of three.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene rats_in_the_eaves
when topic=animals|home self.dislikes=rats
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack
a : There's a rat in the eaves. I heard it at midnight, walking about like it paid the rent. | I found teeth marks on a crock this morning. Through the bladder, stopped at the wax.
b : Have you set a trap? | So the wax held?
a : Two traps and a cat on loan. The cat is not interested. The rat is very interested. | The wax held. The rat learned nothing, I expect, and will be back tonight with friends.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_bad_luck#

scene flies_on_the_jam
when topic=animals|weather here.season=summer self.dislikes=flies
does tell.complaint / react.complaint.agree / tell.self / ack
a : Flies. I've hung muslin over every crock and they're queuing at the corners like it's a toll gate. | Summer's lovely, except for the flies. I'd give up the warm for a winter without flies.
b : They're terrible this year. | You'd give up the summer?
a : Every year's terrible. I keep a swatter by the scales and I'm getting quicker. | Not quite. But I'd want a word with whoever designed flies.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene mutton_stew_night
when topic=food here.season=autumn|winter here.time=dusk|night
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : #stew.capitalize#, on the hob since noon. Winter's whole reason, and I'll fight anyone on it. | The stewpot's been thinking about itself all afternoon.
b : I could eat that now. | Thinking about itself?
a : Bring your own bowl and you're welcome. Mine stays mine. | Simmering. Longer it thinks, better it tastes. Wolves are similar.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene draughts_at_dusk
when topic=pastimes here.time=dusk|night self.likes=draughts
does invite / accept_invite / tease / tease.return
a : A game, if you've time? The board's out and the kettle's on. | One game. Just one. I promise to lose politely if you promise to win quietly.
b : One game, then. Just one. | I'll beat you this time, you'll see.
a : One game. As if it's ever one game. Sit. Black or white? | Brave talk. Your last three games say otherwise, but we'll let the board decide.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_we_will_see#

scene draughts_strategy
when topic=pastimes self.likes=draughts
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : The whole game of draughts is in the back row. Leave it alone and you can't lose quickly. | I've played the same old sailor every Restday for years. He's never won a game, and he's never stopped coming. | I keep a chipped king on #board# for luck. Lost the proper one down the stairs.
b : And if you can't lose quickly? | Why does he keep coming? | Does it bring luck?
a : Then you can only lose slowly, and slow losing is half a win. | For the company, and the dried apples I put out. I'd come too, for those. | It's brought me eleven wins this month. I've stopped questioning it.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene tidy_loft
when topic=home|smalltalk self.likes=tidy_house
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Every crock has its spot on the shelf, and I could find each one blind. | Swept the stairs twice today. Dust first, then pleasure.
b : Hard work, keeping it so. | Twice?
a : Less work than finding a crock in a mess. Tidy's lazy, done properly. | Twice. I'd have done it three times, but I ran out of daylight and the broom ran out of patience.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_ha#

scene first_warm_day_windows
when topic=weather|season here.season=spring here.weather=clear self.likes=first_warm_day
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.plan / ack
a : First warm day. Every loft window's open, and my chest's gone quiet at last. | Smell that? Dry air. If it went in a jar I'd sell it.
b : You'll be glad of it. | You'd sell it, too.
a : Glad isn't the word. I'm going to air every string in the loft and sit on the step like a cat. | I would. A penny a jar. Nobody'd buy it in spring, everybody would in winter.
b : #bc_lucky_you# | #bc_ha#

scene child_and_the_jam
when topic=family self.parent=yes self.child self.role=merchant
does tell.feeling / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I'd let #self.child# taste every batch, and I'd want the truth back. It'd be the sugar, I expect. | I'd hoped #self.child# would take on the loft. We'll see. You can't make a preserver; you can only leave the jam out. | #self.child# gets more of my worrying than shows. Not much more. Some.
b : Would you listen? | Would that be so bad, if not? | That's natural, isn't it?
a : I'd sulk a day, then put a pinch less in the next one. | No. Better a happy wolf at another trade than a sour one in my loft. | Natural, aye. Doesn't make me sleep any better.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_wise# | #bc_true#

scene friend_trusted_with_keys
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I'd give #self.friend# the key to the loft and not think twice. That's the whole list. | #self.friend# and I have years between us, more than we'd admit to.
b : That's a short list. | That's good, to have somebody like that.
a : Short and tidy. Like the loft. | Aye. Worth more than a full larder, some winters.

scene travellers_for_the_road
when topic=player|newcomer self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : A traveller bought ten strings of apple rings this morning for the road. Asked if they'd keep to the coast. | Folk passing through always want something that keeps. I'm the shop for them.
b : And will they keep? | Do they come back?
a : To the coast and home again, provided nobody sits on them. I said so, too. | Some. Returners always bring news. Some days I'd take the news over the coin.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_good#

scene apple_carts_late
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.job=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / tell.self / ack
a : Orchard carts behind again. Each day out there bruises the apples a bit more. | Seven days and no apples. I'm drying my last crate and pretending I meant to.
b : They'll be here soon. | You'll manage till then.
a : I hope so. Bruised I can work with. Rotten I can't. | Manage, aye. Jam can be stretched. So can I.
b : #bc_hope_so# | #bc_ha#

scene apple_carts_in
when topic=caravan world.wagons=in self.job=merchant
does tell.news / react.news / tell.plan / ack
a : Carts in. Twelve baskets climbing my stairs today, and two strong backs promised to carry them. | Apples in, at last. I'll be fuming and threading till midnight, and glad of it.
b : That's good. You'll be busy. | Till midnight?
a : Busy and sticky and happy. That's my favourite state of all three. | Midnight, aye. Apples don't wait for a sensible hour.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_ha#

scene ships_not_my_subject
when topic=ships here.region=ridgemere self.age>=40
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : I don't follow the ships. Ask somebody who's fond of the wharf. | Ships. Pick me another subject, any other.
b : I didn't know you felt so strongly. | Something happened down there?
a : I do. Enough said. Talk to me about apples. | Long ago. Grayrock's cranes, and a friend from my pup days. No more than that.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_im_sorry#

scene gossip_with_a_dry_eye
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes
does tell.gossip / react.news / tell.self / ack
a : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. It came over the scales between the plums and the pickles. | Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'd take it with a grain of sugar. | I'll tell you what's going round, and you can decide: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#.
b : Do you believe it? | A grain of sugar? | What do you think?
a : I believe somebody said it. Beyond that, I'm a preserver, not a judge. | Sweeter than salt, but it still keeps a story going. | I think the wolf who told me had a full basket and wanted to stay talking. Weigh that how you like.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene gossip_death_kept_quiet
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=death
does tell.gossip / sympathise / assess
a : Word reached me that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. A sorry business. | They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've nothing clever to add to that.
b : That's sad news. | It's hard, when you hear it secondhand.
a : Sad, aye. A crock goes round to the family. It's what I do; it's about all I can. | It is. Secondhand, and still it lands in the chest.

scene hard_times_crocks
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Hard times are odd for me. Nobody buys jam, but everybody buys dried apples. They keep. | When folk are short, they stop buying sweet and start buying what lasts. My loft does both, luckily.
b : So you're doing all right? | Lucky you.
a : I'm doing tidy. Not well, not badly. Tidy. | Lucky and careful. Each good autumn sets a little aside for a winter like this one.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_wise#

scene festival_jam_stall
when topic=festival world.festival=today|soon self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / tell.plan / ack
a : Festival day. I'll set a table outside with small pots of jam and sell out by noon. | Everybody out in the square, and no one rushing. Festivals are good for that.
b : You'll be busy, then. | You'll come out and dance?
a : Busy till noon, and then I'll stand in the square eating my own jam and watching the fools dance. | Dance? With this chest? I'll watch. Watching's free and I don't cough doing it.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene winter_stores_selling
when topic=season|work here.season=winter self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Winter's when the loft pays for itself. No fresh fruit anywhere, and folk remember I've got some put by. | Every crock I sealed in autumn, I sell in winter, crock by crock, to folk who laughed at me in autumn.
b : Do you run out? | Who laughed at you?
a : Never quite. I keep the last crate for spring, in case. Nobody's ever needed it. Next year, maybe. | Everyone who says jam's a lot of fuss. They come in by midwinter, quiet as mice.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_ha#

scene loft_at_night
when topic=work|home here.time=night self.job=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : At night the loft creaks like a ship, and every creak's a rat until it isn't. | I go up and count the crocks before bed. It's soothing. I lose count at forty and start again.
b : That'd keep me up. | Why start again?
a : A whole year of bad sleep. Now nothing wakes me but a squeak. | Because forty is where it gets interesting. After forty I start to wonder if I miscounted at twenty.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene accent_none
when topic=accent
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Town folk reckon I sound like the middle of the Commons. I'd rather sound understood. | I've no accent, apparently. I've got a chest, and a lot of opinions, but no accent.
b : You do speak very plainly. | No harm in that, surely.
a : I like plain. Plain keeps. Fancy goes off by spring. | Not in my trade. A customer understands every word, and pays every penny.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_true_enough#

scene faith_in_the_loft
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : A dry autumn gets thanked for. Out loud, in the loft, to the Bright One, with only apples listening. | Saint I'm not. Still, no crock gets sealed here without a quick prayer over it, just in case.
b : I'm sure the apples appreciate it. | Just in case of what?
a : They've never complained. My customers could learn from them. | In case of mould. The Bright One's kept the mould off so far, and long may that hold.

scene curious_questions
when topic=smalltalk|day
does ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self / ack
a : Tell me something I don't know. Anything. I've been up in the loft all morning with nobody but the flies. | What have you seen today? I've seen forty strings of apples and a broom.
b : I've nothing new, I'm afraid. | Not much. Rain, mostly.
a : Then tell me something old. Old's fine. I've been talking to apples since breakfast. | Rain, mostly. Well, that's honest. I've seen the same, through a small window.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_true#

story the_crane_and_the_wharf [topic=memories|ships self.age>=40 here.region=ridgemere]
  : There was a pup I ran the piers with, down by the water, before either of us had any sense.
  : He went to work for Grayrock at fifteen. A crane rope parted his first winter, and that was that.
  : Grayrock sent a basket of fruit to his mother. Fruit. I've sold fruit half my life since, and the basket's still not forgiven.
  ask : In their place, what would you have sent her?
  b[old] : Nothing. Myself, maybe. I'd have gone and sat with her.
  b : Not fruit. Something that meant something.
  : That's it. That's all anyone needed. They sent a basket.

story the_first_spoiled_batch [topic=work|memories self.job=merchant self.age>=25]
  : The first winter I sold preserves, I sealed forty crocks a day too early, while they were still cool.
  : By midwinter every one had a grey beard under the wax. Forty crocks.
  ask : Guess where those forty went?
  b : Threw them out and started again?
  b[old] : Fed them to somebody's pigs, I'd bet.
  : Neither. I scraped the beards off, boiled them up again, and sold them as winter jam. Nobody died. I've sealed everything hot since.

story the_rat_in_the_crock [topic=animals|memories self.age>=20]
  : I opened a crock of dried apples once, in the dark, and something in it moved.
  : A rat. It had eaten its way in through the side, eaten its fill, and was too fat to get back out.
  ask : A fat rat in your best crock: your move?
  b : Dropped the crock and run, I think.
  : I put the lid back on and carried the whole crock to the river. It was a good crock. I still miss it more than I miss the rat.

story the_winter_the_city_came [topic=memories|season self.age>=30 self.role=merchant]
  : One winter the ice came in so hard that nothing reached the city for a month. No carts, no ships.
  : Folk came up my stairs in a line that went round the corner. I sold every string and every crock I had.
  : I could have tripled the price. Some told me to. I didn't.
  ask : Would you have, in my place?
  b : Maybe a little. It's hard to say.
  : Each winter since, I've turned it over again. I'd do the same again. They still buy from me, every one of them.

story draughts_with_the_sailor [topic=pastimes|memories self.age>=25 self.likes=draughts]
  : An old sailor used to play me at draughts on Restdays, and he cheated. Moved my pieces when I looked away.
  : I knew. I let him. One Restday he forgot to cheat and lost, and he glared, as though I'd picked his pocket.
  ask : Why do you think I let him?
  b : So he'd keep coming back?
  : That, and he brought the best tobacco on the street. A wolf can forgive a lot for good company and a decent smell.

rule @bark_strings
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.role=merchant self.job=merchant
does aside
cooldown 7h
say Dried apples, by the string. Keep till spring. | Jam, sealed hot. Plum and apple, both.

rule @bark_chest
when concept=bark here.weather=rain|fog
does aside
cooldown 9h
say There it goes. Rain by noon. | Hush, chest. I heard you.

rule @bark_flies
when concept=bark here.season=summer self.dislikes=flies
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Off the jam, you. | Another fly. I'll have that one.

rule @bark_rats
when concept=bark here.time=night self.dislikes=rats
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Was that a squeak? | I hear you up there. The trap's baited, friend.

rule @bark_sweep
when concept=bark self.likes=tidy_house
does aside
cooldown 11h
say Who walked mud up my stairs? | There. Tidy. Leave it like that.

rule @bark_stew
when concept=bark here.time=dusk self.likes=mutton_stew
does aside
cooldown 12h
say That stew should be about ready. | Smell that. Mutton and barley.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. Mind the strings, they hang low. What's wanted? | #greet#. Smells of apples, I know. What is it?
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. Good, a familiar face. Any news on you? | #greet#, #topic.player#. There's a stool. What brings you?
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Late. I was counting crocks. Quick, before I forget the number. | Dark out, and I'm yawning, but go on.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Stay near the doorway, please. Away from the racks. What is it? | You've a sharp look. Say it, and then we'll judge.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=merchant self.role=merchant] : I dry apples and seal jam, and I sell what keeps. Everything in the loft lasts till spring. | Preserving. Apples on strings, fruit in crocks, and a lot of sulphur in between.
dlg @2 resident.job : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Busy work, and the city eats from it in winter. | I keep things from spoiling. Fruit, mostly. Tempers, when I can.
dlg @3 resident.job [self.working=yes] : Working. You can talk while I thread. | I'm at it now. Mind the sulphur pot, it's warm.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Thriving. Crocks go out the door warm from the wax. | Very good. Best year the loft's had.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. Tidy. Rent met, loft dry. | Middling. There's always a sweet tooth somewhere, and a winter coming.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Badly. Nobody's buying, and the loft doesn't stay dry for free. | Poorly, I'll be honest. I'm eating more of the stock than I sell.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Word's come from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. A traveller paid for apples with it. | From #world.news_from#: #world.news#. That's the talk at the counter.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : It's #world.event_words#, all day, every customer. I'm learning more than I wanted. | Everybody's full of #world.event_words#. I've heard it nine times by noon.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Robbers on the road. A wagon taken. I hope it wasn't my apples, but I'd bet it was. | Somebody's wagon was stopped past the gate. Bide indoors, I would.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.death=recent] : Somebody's passed, near here. I sent a crock round. Small comfort, but honest. | We lost a neighbour this week. Curtains drawn all along the row.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : Somebody's married! Plum jam sold out by noon because of it. | Wedding bells near here, lately. I watched from the back row, grinning.
dlg @6 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Unless you count my chest, which says rain. | Nothing new. The apples are drying and the world's carrying on.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Repeated as received, mind. | Word at the counter is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Take it with a grain of sugar.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted] : They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'm locking the loft at night. | Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Coin in the inside pocket, I'd say.
dlg @3 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : Nothing worth repeating. I've been up in the loft with the apples. | I've heard nothing, and I listen better than most.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Mind the strings on the way out. | #farewell#. Close the door; the damp's always looking for a way in.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=night] : Good night. I've crocks to count. | Night. The gutter at the stair's foot is deeper than it looks.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare self.job=merchant] : #greet#. Dried apples by the string, jam by the pot, all of it keeping till spring. | #greet#. Have a look. Everything here was picked sound and sealed hot.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. Have a look round. The prices are marked plain. | #greet#. Take your time. It all keeps, so there's no hurry.
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. Back for more? A string's been hanging aside for you. | #greet#, #topic.player#. My stairs know your step now.
dlg @4 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #greet#. The loft's empty till the next carts come. Got fruit to sell? I'm listening. | Bare shelves, I'm afraid. The apples ran out before the winter did.
dlg @1 shop.prices : Sugar's up, so jam's up. Apples are steady, the rest is as marked. | It's all marked. If it's dear, blame the sugar, not me.
dlg @1 shop.bye : #farewell#. Dry shelf, cool corner, and it'll see out the winter. | #shop_bye# Lid on tight, and it'll see you to spring.
dlg @1 master.learn [here.teaches=yes self.job=merchant] : Preserving? Learning to make things last. A sound ambition. Ask me anything. | Apples, sugar and patience, and patience is the one you can't buy. Ask away.
dlg @2 master.learn [here.teaches=yes] : #here.trade_title.capitalize#? A good trade, and a quiet one. Ask what you like. | It's worth learning. Ask, and I'll answer straight.
dlg @3 master.learn [here.teaches=no] : One learner's already under my feet, and one's plenty. Questions cost nothing, though. | My learner's place is filled. Ask anything you like, all the same.
dlg @1 master.work : Early starts, sticky paws and four flights to climb. And the sulphur, which you'll learn to love or leave. | Long days in autumn, short ones in spring. Steady work, honest work.
dlg @1 master.wages : No pay for learning, I'm afraid. Stand at my side and it comes quick, and the jars you fill are yours to sell. | No wages. What you learn is the wage, and what you sell is yours.
dlg @1 master.takes [here.take_on=yes] : Just the one learner, and trusted. You'd suit. Ask, and it's done. | I'd take you. You strike me as a wolf who sweeps up after.
dlg @2 master.takes [here.take_on=no] : I'm choosy about who stands at my racks. #here.take_on_why# | Not just yet. #here.take_on_why#
dlg @1 master.refused : #topic.why# Come back when that's changed. | Not now. #topic.why#
graft resident.hub
choice That sounds like a bad cough. -> chest
node chest
say Not a cough. Weather. My chest goes thick before rain and clears when it's dry. Better than any glass on a sea captain's wall.
mood calm
free
choice So what's it saying now? -> chest_now
choice Back to it. -> hub
node chest_now
say Rain, likely, by evening. It's always rain in this city, so I'm rarely wrong. But it knows the hour, and that's the clever bit.
choice I'll take a hood, then. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft master.learn
choice How do you keep apples from going brown? [self.job=merchant] -> brown
node brown
say Cut them a finger thick, drop them in lime water quick, then fume them over sulphur before they go up on the racks. Colour stays, flies stay off.
mood keen
free
choice Is the sulphur safe? -> brown_safe
choice Back to the lesson. -> learn
node brown_safe
say Safe for the apples. Less so for the chest. Keep the window open and stand upwind of the pot.
choice Back to the lesson. -> learn
choice Goodbye. -> bye
